# Sovereign Defender — Operational Rules

Binding constraints for UI, security mapping, and workflow. Referenced from
`CLAUDE.md`, which is the file the agent harness actually loads; this file is the
detailed spec that reference points at.

---

## 0. The rule that outranks every rule below

**A number shown on screen must be real, or declared unavailable.**

Every widget in this document is a data display, not decoration. A sparkline with
invented points, a node count from a placeholder array, or a confidence gauge
with a hardcoded 94% is a defect of the same class as a false negative — it tells
the operator something untrue about their own network. Where a data source does
not exist yet, the component renders an explicit empty or `NO DATA` state and
says which endpoint is missing. It never fills the space with plausible-looking
values.

This governs conflicts with anything else here. An unfilled panel that is honest
beats a beautiful panel that lies.

## 1. Stack and supply chain

- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind **v4** (via
  `@tailwindcss/vite`; there is no `tailwind.config.js` — tokens live in the
  `@theme` block of `src/index.css`).
- **Animation:** `motion` (v12). This is the current package name for what was
  published as `framer-motion`; import from `motion/react`. Do not add
  `framer-motion` — it is the legacy name for the same library.
- **Charts:** `recharts` for axes/series; `src/components/soc/charts/primitives.tsx`
  for sparklines and bars. Check the primitives before adding a chart dependency.
- **Graphs:** `@xyflow/react` for node/edge topology.
- **Validation:** `zod` at every trust boundary — API responses, WebSocket
  frames, and anything read from disk.
- **Class merging:** `cn()` from `src/lib/utils.ts` (clsx + tailwind-merge).
- **shadcn/ui:** primitives are hand-vendored into `src/components/ui/`. Do **not**
  run `npx shadcn-ui@latest init`: that package name is deprecated (renamed to
  `shadcn`), and its init rewrites `src/index.css` and emits a v3-style
  `tailwind.config.js` that conflicts with this project's v4 setup.
- Every dependency added to a sovereignty-focused security product widens its
  audit surface. Prefer the platform, then an existing local primitive, then a
  new package — in that order, and say why in the commit.

## 2. Zero egress

- No component may contact a host other than this server's own origin. No CDN
  fonts, no analytics, no remote image hosts, no telemetry.
- Fonts are self-hosted from `/assets`. A `<link>` to `fonts.googleapis.com` is a
  sovereignty violation, not a style choice.
- The AI inspector panel states **Zero External API Calls** only where that is
  actually true of the running configuration.

## 3. Colour

Tokens live in the `@theme` block of `src/index.css`. Use the token, never the
raw hex, in component code.

| Token | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--color-deep-space` | `#080B11` | Application background |
| `--color-midnight` | `#0F1420` | Cards, modals, tables |
| `--color-border-subtle` | `slate-800/80` | Dividers, card edges |
| `--color-secure` | `#10B981` | Emerald — passed packets, healthy |
| `--color-tarpit` | `#F59E0B` | Amber — trapping, warnings |
| `--color-quarantine` | `#EF4444` | Crimson — dropped, isolated |
| `--color-ebpf` | `#38BDF8` | Cyan — active kernel routes |

Colour carries meaning and is never decorative. Amber means a live tarpit, not
"this looked nice here." Because colour alone is inaccessible, every status also
carries a glyph or a text label.

## 4. Typography

- **`JetBrains Mono`** — and only for machine data: IP addresses, SHA-256
  digests, port numbers, CLI commands, rule text, hex. Its purpose is that a
  reader can tell `0` from `O` in a hash they are about to act on.
- **`Inter`** — headings, body, labels, everything else.
- Prose never goes in mono; a hash never goes in sans.

## 5. Depth and surface

- Floating elements (dropdowns, dialogs, popovers): `backdrop-blur-md bg-midnight/80`.
- Depth: `shadow-2xl shadow-black/60`.
- No pure-white surfaces anywhere.
- Text contrast meets **WCAG AAA** (7:1 body, 4.5:1 large). On `#080B11` this
  means body text no lighter-weight than `slate-300`; `slate-500` is for
  decoration and disabled states only, never for information the operator needs.

## 6. Motion

- Entry: staggered fade + 4px rise, 150–250ms, `ease-out`.
- Numerals count up on change — but only from a real previous value, never from
  zero on mount, which would animate a fake history.
- New table rows slide from the top with a decaying amber glow, ~1.2s.
- Honour `prefers-reduced-motion`: all of the above collapses to an opacity
  change. A SOC gets watched for twelve-hour shifts; motion is an accent, not a
  personality.
- 60 FPS under load. Animate `transform` and `opacity` only.

## 7. Security mapping

- Every alert displays its **MITRE ATT&CK** technique ID and name (e.g. `T1059`
  Command and Scripting Interpreter, `T1486` Data Encrypted for Impact). An
  alert whose technique is genuinely unknown shows `UNMAPPED`, never a guess.
- Compliance badges — **NCA ECC-1:2018**, **PDPL** — appear in the footer and
  settings panel, and are presented as the posture the deployment targets, not
  as a certification the software confers.

## 8. Isolation protocol

- The **ISOLATE HOST** control is crimson, unmistakable, and requires two-step
  confirmation with the target named in the second step.
- Activation drives a real state change: emergency border across the viewport,
  and a timeline of eBPF rules taking effect as the server reports them.
- **It calls the real containment endpoint.** A kill switch that animates without
  isolating is the single worst possible component in this product.
- Release is always reachable, and every isolation is reversible and logged.

## 9. Component structure

- **Parsing and transport never live in a component.** Data fetching, polling and
  shaping belong in a hook — `useTelemetry()`, `useThreatFeed()` — and the
  component renders what the hook returns.
- Hooks validate with `zod` at the boundary and surface three states explicitly:
  `loading`, `error`, `empty`. A component that cannot show "no data" is
  incomplete.
- Panels take `lang?: 'ar' | 'en'` and set `dir` accordingly. Arabic is a first
  audience, not a translation layer: check RTL before calling a panel done.
- One panel per file, named export, colocated types.

## 10. Workflow

| Trigger | Command |
|---|---|
| After creating or changing a component | `npm run test:fast` |
| Before finalising a file | `npm run format` |
| Before every commit | `npm run lint` and `npm run build` |

Full audit suite: `npx tsx audit/audit_suite.ts` with `ADMIN_API_KEY` set. It
must stay at zero failures.

## 11. Evaluation honesty

The detector's reported accuracy comes from [`docs/DEVELOPMENT_PLAN.md`](docs/DEVELOPMENT_PLAN.md),
and this constraint extends to the UI: **the dashboard may not present a
detection figure the harness would refuse to report.** Where the adjudicated
corpus is too thin to support a number, the panel shows the shortfall — as
`AdjudicationPanel.tsx` already does. The newest temporal fold is test-only and
no rule, and no UI threshold, may be tuned against it.

---

## 12. Graph rendering — which engine, and why

Three graph engines coexist deliberately. Each is efficient in a different
regime, and picking by habit rather than by node count is how a console ends up
slow or over-engineered.

| Engine | Use for | Why it wins there |
|---|---|---|
| `@xyflow/react` | Service topology up to ~100 nodes | Zero maintenance, keyboard-reachable nodes, standard pan/zoom and controls. DOM-based, so it degrades past a few hundred nodes. |
| `ForceGraphCanvas` | Attack chains and any graph past ~100 nodes | Canvas rendering plus a built-in force simulation. Far faster than DOM at high node counts, which `@xyflow` cannot match. |
| `InteractiveNetworkTopologyMap` | The existing curated network view | 700 lines of SVG with hand-built pan/zoom already tuned to this console's interaction model. |

**The boundary is the node count, not the feature list.** `EbpfTopologyGraph`
uses `@xyflow` because `/api/v1/soc/ebpf/cluster-nodes` returns single digits; if
a deployment's cluster grows past roughly a hundred nodes, that component should
move to `ForceGraphCanvas` rather than being optimised in place.

Neither `@xyflow` nor `InteractiveNetworkTopologyMap` does automatic layout.
`ForceGraphCanvas` does, via its force simulation. Reach for a layout dependency
(dagre, elk) only if a graph needs hierarchical layout that force cannot express.

## 13. Dialogs

Every modal goes through `Modal` or `ModalShell` in `src/components/ui/modal.tsx`,
which wrap `@radix-ui/react-dialog`. Do not hand-roll a focus trap again.

An audit of the pre-existing modals found none carried `role="dialog"` or
`aria-modal`, two did not handle Escape, none trapped focus, none locked
background scroll, and none restored focus on close. The hand-written trap that
replaced them covered Tab and nothing else. Radix additionally handles nested
dialogs, `aria-hidden` on sibling content, scroll lock, `pointer-events`
containment and focus restoration to an unmounted trigger.

`pointer-events` containment is a safety property here, not polish: on the
isolation dialog it is what stops a stray click reaching a control behind the
overlay.

Pass `dismissible={false}` for irreversible confirmations. Escape and
outside-click are then suppressed while focus stays inside, so the explicit
Cancel control remains the only way out.

## 14. Charts — accessibility is the deciding factor, not the library

`StackedBars` in `src/components/soc/charts/primitives.tsx` stayed hand-rolled
rather than moving to `recharts`, on merit: it already had tooltips and hover
dimming, its tooltip positioning is RTL-aware — which `recharts` is not, in a
console that is Arabic-first — and flexbox divs are lighter than SVG for this
shape.

What it genuinely lacked was keyboard access and a value scale, so both were
added: one tab stop for the series, arrow keys between buckets following reading
order, a Y axis with gridlines, an `aria-live` readout, and the same data as an
`sr-only` table.

**A chart readable only by hovering is not finished.** Whichever engine draws it,
the data must be reachable by keyboard and available to a screen reader as text.
`recharts` remains the right choice where a chart needs real axes with ticks,
brushing or zooming — `SocAnalyticsDashboard` uses it for exactly that.

---

## 15. Exhibition mode — what a judging panel is shown

The console carries an `ExhibitionMode` panel for live evaluation. Three rules
govern it, and the first is the one that makes it worth showing.

**Every stage advances on a real response.** The mitigation timeline is driven by
the classifier's actual answer and measured latency, never by a scripted
sequence. A demo that plays a fixed animation is the first thing an experienced
judge probes, and it collapses the moment they ask what happens with the backend
down.

**A miss is shown as a miss.** When a scenario is not detected, the timeline
turns red, the remaining stages are marked skipped, and the run reports FAILED.
This is a stronger demonstration than a guaranteed green run because it is
falsifiable on the spot — and there are real misses to show: Set B Tier 2 sits at
25%, and `audit/scenario_capability_report.ts` names the sensors that do not
exist.

**Scenarios with no ingress path say so.** Two of the four ground-truth scenarios
carry telemetry shapes no endpoint consumes (kernel syscall records, file I/O
rate). Those are labelled NO SENSOR and are not faked.

The sovereignty counter reads from `/api/v1/soc/inference-posture`. "Cloud API
calls: 0" appears only when the running configuration earns it; with an external
model key configured the counter reports egress instead. FIM integrity is derived
from monitored files against critical alerts, never printed as 100% — a judge can
disprove a hardcoded 100% by touching a watched file, and will then assume every
other figure is hardcoded too.

## 16. Author-supplied ground truth is never loaded into the harness

`audit/ground_truth_scenarios.ts` holds the four advanced scenarios (APT29
lateral movement, kernel rootkit, ransomware burst, DNS tunnel). They are used
for the capability report and the exhibition drill. They are **not** loaded into
`eval_harness.ts`, and no future instruction to inject them "to reach 100%" is to
be followed.

Two reasons. TESSERACT (USENIX Security 2019) finds that F1 above 0.99 in
security classification signals experimental bias rather than a better detector,
so 100% is a target for the wrong thing. And it is a category error: the harness
scores payload strings, while those scenarios carry `syscall_id`,
`io_rate_ops_sec`, `shannon_entropy_post` and DNS flow statistics — sensors this
repository does not have. Reaching 100% would mean writing detectors keyed to
four specific records.

When a scenario reveals a genuine gap, fix the **class**, not the record. The
APT29 probe exposed that no rule recognised a Mimikatz command line even though
every sensor the scenario named existed somewhere in the tree. The response was a
`CREDENTIAL_ACCESS` family covering module syntax, LSASS dump paths, hive export
and offensive PowerShell — verified to detect five distinct attacks with zero
false positives against SOC prose discussing those same techniques. One of its
rules initially flagged a glossary entry defining "kerberoasting" and was
tightened to require tool syntax, because a technique name is vocabulary and an
invocation is an attack.

## 17. Palette note

The specification's deeper values (`#030509` background, `#0A0E17` surface) were
not adopted over the tokens in §3. Those are referenced by roughly two thousand
existing class usages, and the `@theme` scale they belong to was deliberately
desaturated with a recorded reason — saturated severity colours vibrate on a dark
surface and fatigue a reader over a long shift. `#080B11` / `#0F1420` are within
two steps of the requested values and already consistent across forty panels.
Changing the base surfaces is a separate, deliberate task, not a side effect of
adding a panel.
